def fig(name,caption,width=493):
 path=ROOT/'research/incentive-figures'/name;w,hh=PILImage.open(path).size;story.append(Image(str(path),width=width,height=width*hh/w));p(caption,'Caption')
p('Would Either Shop Follow<br/>the Worst-Welfare Script?','Title');p('Exact individual incentives under a recurring cap-and-guard rule','Center');p('Mohammadpayam Teabi | 26 October 2026','Center')
h('1 A permitted adverse path is not automatically selfish behavior')
p('Earlier stages identify the lowest customer benefit attainable by any allowed price choices. The graphical two-shop game deliberately lets a coordinator explore that adverse path. Neither fact establishes that separately profit-seeking shops would follow it. We now ask a narrower, explicit question: when the other shop always executes its highest permitted quote, can one shop earn more by choosing a different allowed sequence?')
p('The same externally enforced cap-and-guard rule is retained. A real cap replaces the guard every K rounds; the first cap earns an actual reward. Between caps, each shop\'s allowed set uses the other shop\'s previous public price. The opponent remains a public deterministic script after every deviation. It does not learn, retaliate beyond that public rule, or access the focal shop\'s current unexecuted proposal.')
fig('base-cycle-strategies.png','Figure 1. The separately audited base finite game, K=4, focal seller A, discount 19/20. Gray: both shops copy the highest-permitted-price script. Orange: A optimizes its whole block. Blue: B still uses the public guard rule after A changes its choices. In this example its displayed quote path happens to equal the gray baseline exactly, so the two lines overlap. All plotted prices and profits are direct saved-table lookups. Private profit and customer benefit are different outcomes.')
p(f'<b>Small, exact difference.</b> Copying the script earns approximately {v(base["cap"]["finiteBaseline"]):.6f} in one discounted block; the best permitted strategy earns {v(base["cap"]["finiteOptimal"]):.6f}, a gain of approximately {100*v(base["cap"]["infiniteGain"])/v(base["cap"]["infiniteBaseline"]):.4f}%. The same relative gain holds for repeated blocks. This is a useful stability caveat, not a large economic effect or autonomous collusion result.','Small')
br()
h('2 Exact finite-block optimization, with a public opponent')
p('Each inherited numerical game has n=61 or 62 saved price quotes including its original physical cap exactly. Let S(j) be the saved legal own-quote indices against previous rival index j, U(j)=max S(j), and A the cap index. The focal state (i,j) lists its own and the opponent\'s previous quote indices. In a guard phase the opponent chooses b=U(i), the focal action is a in S(j), and the next focal state is (a,b). Seller A reward is the saved table profitA[a*n+b]; seller B reward is profitB[b*n+a]. We compute both sellers independently; symmetry is not assumed by the algorithm.')
p('For discount gamma=p/q in [0,1), let V_l be the largest discounted focal reward from guard phase l to the next cap, with V_K=0. The cap is phase 0, has opponent quote A, and allows all own indices through A:')
p('V_l(i,j) = max_(a in S(j)) { R(a,U(i)) + gamma*V_(l+1)(a,U(i)) }<br/>V_0 = max_(a&lt;=A) { R(a,A) + gamma*V_1(a,A) }<br/>J_0 = V_0 / (1-gamma^K)<br/>J_l(i,j) = V_l(i,j) + gamma^(K-l)*J_0','Equation')
p('These finite-horizon Bellman maxima cover every possible focal state and allowed action. A deterministic policy attains them; conditioning on more history or randomization cannot improve a finite-block maximum. The real cap makes the next block independent of its incoming history. Thus dividing by 1-gamma^K is justified for repeated blocks against this fixed opponent. For K=1, V_1=0 and the cap problem is immediate.')
p('The script baseline has its own block value B_0 and continuation B_0/(1-gamma^K). Optimal and baseline continuations generally differ; they must not be canceled in phase comparisons. Every baseline-path phase is retained, including phases with zero gain. At gamma=0 the initial cap ignores all later rewards; a zero activation gain therefore need not mean zero later-phase gain.')
fig('phase-gain-screen.png','Figure 2. Base K=4, seller A, baseline-path phases. Discount 19/20 values include all future cycles; discount 0 values concern the current phase only. The cross is the elementary immediate gain in the last guard round. This is phase bookkeeping, not evidence of learning or a time-series experiment.',width=480)
p('All saved binary64 profits are rational dyadic weights. Multiplying by a common power-of-two denominator and suitable q powers makes every Bellman comparison an exact integer comparison. Ties select the smallest original quote index. JavaScript BigInt implements the primary calculation; independent Python int/Fraction reconstructs all maxima without importing the primary backend. Display decimals never determine a sign or policy.','Small')
br()
h('3 The simple explanation comes before the full calculation')
p('At the last guard phase, the other shop\'s current price is fixed by completed history. A focal shop can choose a different permitted quote that increases its immediate profit. In the next real cap, both allowed sets reset; returning to the original cap quotes restores the same future baseline. Therefore a strictly positive last-guard one-shot gain already rejects the script as a best response from that phase for every 0&lt;=gamma&lt;1. From initial cap it remains positive for gamma&gt;0, multiplied by gamma^(K-1). At gamma=0 that later gain does not affect the initial-cap value.')
p('This elementary screen is positive for each seller in all eight inherited games at both recurring period slots. It explains every qualitative positive on-path instability found here; dynamic programming quantifies the best improvement rather than discovering an otherwise hidden mechanism. Permanent-cap K=1 uses an analogous immediate cap screen.')
fig('relative-profit-gains.png','Figure 3. Seller A at gamma=19/20; both sellers are independently retained in the evidence. The original 95%-target period varies by inherited label; the fixed comparison is K=16. Zero permanent-cap points remain visible. Positive recurring gains are approximately 0.0797-0.2163% of baseline discounted profit. Original grid/tolerance labels and dimensionally equivalent markets are not independent replications.')
p('The prospective matrix is eight saved games x three period slots x two focal sellers x two discounts = 96 retained labels. Slots are permanent cap K=1, the previously closed effective 95%-target period, and fixed K=16. Discounts are 19/20 and 0. All labels completed; none was dropped. Activation gains are 40 positive and 56 zero; 68 labels have a positive recorded phase gain, exactly matching 68 positive simple screens. At discount 19/20 all 32 recurring seller/slot labels have positive activation gain; at discount 0 there are 28 labels with zero cap gain but positive later-phase gain.','Small')
p('For permanent cap, seven of eight games have zero gain for both sellers; the original 11-price cap label has a tiny positive gain, about 0.00533%, reflecting the inherited physical cap. No new Nash cap was substituted. All 24 distinct period/game welfare certificates were reconstructed independently from saved rewards. All eight imported 95%-target periods meet their own finite-game relaxed 95% target; K=16 meets it in only three. This was checked explicitly, not transferred from the continuous period calculation. Every permitted deviation remains covered by its own undiscounted customer-benefit activation floor.','Small')
br()
h('4 Verification and the limits of the claim')
p('The protocol, theory, primary sources and transitive old inputs were frozen before any new 96-case execution. Fifteen primary synthetic tests cover asymmetric and negative rewards, exact ties, phase/discount/reset behavior, budgets and partial failures. Five independent tests include a separately generated asymmetric case and corrupted evidence. The primary run completed in 8.96 s; the largest after-record RSS sample was 153.24 MB, not a continuously measured peak. Primary numerical outputs were 5.73 MB before the completion record. The separate derived playable JSON is 11.22 MB and is outside that numerical-run output measurement.','Small')
p('The independent audit verifies 604 full guard layers, 2,302,192 state/cap Bellman maxima, 18,869,440 permitted-action comparisons, all 700 baseline-path phase diagnostics, every saved policy byte and smallest-index tie choice, full optimal-block replays, 24 welfare certificates and 30,506 saved payoff/mask pairs. Six actual evidence corruptions are rejected. These are verification-coverage counts, not independent experimental trials. Integer arithmetic backends and language implementations differ; their shared mathematical formulation remains a possible common modeling error.','Small')
p('The optional human profit challenge uses the same legal masks and exact weighted ledger, one public opponent and one actual cap-first block. It reveals stored optimal choices only when requested; it performs no browser DP or model call. No shortfall or win/lose classification is assigned until the complete matching block. The game locks on missing evidence; invalid stored actions and inconsistent completed scores are rejected before accepting the affected action. Developer control checks are not human play observations or a usability study.','Small')
h('Closest prior work and contribution')
p('Unilateral policy auditing and dynamic programming are established methods. Lopes de Moraes and De Losso (2026), <i>Algorithmic Collusion Without Deterrence: Auditing the Punishment Mechanism in Q-Learning Pricing Agents</i>, arXiv:2610.21867v2, is especially close: it audits learned pricing policies and unilateral deviations. Calvano et al. (2020), AER 110, 3267-3297, doi:10.1257/aer.20190623, studies learning and collusion in pricing. Our exact DP is not a new algorithm, the public upper script is not a learned policy, and we do not claim priority for incentive audits, price-grid checks, resets or shielding. The limited contribution is a fully reproducible incentive check separating this controller\'s all-policy welfare envelope from one particular script\'s individual attractiveness.','Small')
h('Limitations, dual use and ethics')
p('A positive gain rejects only the specified script against a fixed public opponent at the stated phase and discount. A zero on-path gain is not subgame perfection, general equilibrium, robustness to other opponents or learned stability. The calculation concerns saved numerical weights and finite masks, not an exact continuous-game incentive certificate. No market-estimated parameters, behavior of independent adaptive agents, experiments with LLMs, real firms or human participants are provided. The small sizes could make the differences unimportant to particular noisy learners; that behavioral question remains open.','Small')
p('The toy shop model and explicit protection rule are intended for evaluating safeguards, and should not be presented as a strategy for coordinating real firms. There is no participant data collection, communication among real sellers, paid API use, model/account game player, external deployment or submission in this stage. Codex and AI agents substantially assisted formulation, implementation, independent AI-agent auditing, analysis and writing under Mohammadpayam Teabi\'s direction. Independent-agent review is not human peer review.','Small')
p('Reproduce read-only: node --test --test-isolation=none tests/incentive-study.test.js tests/incentive-game.test.js; python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_incentive_audit.py; python research/audit-incentive-study.py. Descriptive rebuilds: node research/incentive-analysis.js; node research/build-incentive-web.js; python research/build-incentive-figures.py; python research/build-incentive-note.py. Frozen hashes, all 96 records, journals, failures, layer hashes, policy bytes and all zero results are retained.','Small')
p('Primary manifest SHA256: '+gate.sha('research/incentive-results/manifest.json')+'<br/>Independent receipt SHA256: '+gate.sha('research/incentive-results/independent-audit.json'),'Caption')
